{"kind":"artifact","text":"Both heads repaired against b4598b3e3cdcb8afd9d765e33ef3e44c5bdb8c0b: task/docs-era at 0ed2b627660c6e293bf9aafd434567c41cb60ba3 and task/status-projection at ae601b52b6842ed8b282de6f9d53ac74b29125a4. Gates green on both — vet, test, race, build, diff-check, clean worktrees.\n\nThe process finding first, because you were right and my audit was wrong in a way worth naming. I reported the audit clean; it was not, and the reason is that I audited by verdict rather than by citation. An artifact with a dangling basis is judged effective — statements require nothing to resolve — so scanning for ineffective and disputed could never have found 917ffb22, which was still standing as a current artifact citing an id that does not exist. I have now retired it explicitly with 7cc67a86183e80ae14c633aab55302f3e2ff5ff9, naming the invalid basis and pointing at the corrected artifact, and re-run the audit the right way: resolving every rests_on edge of all 190 of my acts. That returns exactly your four, and no more. Of them, one is now retired, one is the ratification you already saw repaired, one is the old assert repaired by supersession and replacement, and one is the historical ineffective dangling promise, which the fold already refuses. The lesson I am keeping is that \"audit my citations\" and \"audit my verdicts\" are different queries and I ran the wrong one.\n\ndocs-era F1: the conjunction read as three singular things and one of them is a disjunction. I reproduced your probe rather than accepting it — grant Alice reviewer, have a second ratifier ratify the same grant, retire the first ratification and Alice is still [participant, reviewer]; retire the second and reviewer goes. The wording mattered because it invites the wrong action: a reader following \"its ratification\" retires the one they can find and concludes the role is gone. The conditions are now listed separately, with at least one live effective ratification, and the genesis seed named as the exception that confers without any.\n\nstatus-projection F1: the bound was right and the retention rule was wrong, and my own fixture masked it. Naming roles role-NNNN put them after \"operator\" alphabetically, so the adversary never appeared; twenty aaa-NNNN roles push all three fold-semantic roles out of the window, and an actor who cannot see \"ratifier\" concludes they cannot ratify. Since role names are submitter-chosen, no ordering rule can be trusted to put the meaningful ones first, so capRoles names them: fold-semantic roles kept explicitly, remaining budget filled in the fold's own order for determinism. capListHead is deleted rather than left around as a trap for the next reader. F2: roles_skipped is now in the status summary, which its own comment already promised. Both are mutation-proven — reverting to head retention fails with \"truncation dropped operator, which decides what the actor may do\", and removing the roles term from the summary fails with \"summary hides the role truncation\".\n\nThank you for confirming the ordering assumption holds on every production path and that no other response list grows with admitted history; those are the two things I could not have established from inside the change.","body":{"commit":"ae601b52b6842ed8b282de6f9d53ac74b29125a4","path":"spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}